{
  "statement": "The Safetyist argument for strict liability and pre-deployment certification gates frames state-administered compliance as an indispensable shield against catastrophic tail risks. That position rightly recognizes that high-autonomy systems can generate diffuse, systemic failures that outpace traditional negligence litigation. Yet treating pre-market vetting as neutral protection ignores how fixed compliance overheads consolidate market power. When regulators mandate static behavioral baselines for autonomous systems, they erect compliance moats that insulate incumbent firms from disruptive startup competition.\n\nRigid liability standards and pre-deployment certification function as anti-competitive barriers that crush small developers and open-source innovation. Historical compliance cost asymmetries in heavily regulated sectors like aviation and pharmaceuticals demonstrate that static regulatory walls lock out nimble entrants and concentrate market power. Under the Food and Drug Administration drug development framework, bringing a single novel compound to market routinely exceeds one billion dollars in compliance and testing expenditures. Applying that same architectural rigidity to autonomous software releases dooms decentralized contributors who lack eighty-person legal teams to interpret ambiguous statutory mandates. Independent artificial intelligence researchers cannot survive strict liability regimes that penalize post-deployment iteration before real-world performance telemetry even materializes.\n\nDynamic market governance via real-time telemetry and private insurance replaces static legal bottlenecks with continuous empirical oversight. High-reliability industries already utilize output-based monitoring and risk-stratified audit requirements rather than blunt, pre-deployment vetoes. Insurance markets routinely price operational risk through continuous data streams in commercial aviation and autonomous logistics fleets operating under federal exemptions. Private underwriters assess fleet telematics down to the millisecond to calibrate coverage costs without freezing code updates behind multi-year administrative reviews. Shifting liability enforcement toward output-based telemetry aligns financial incentives with actual deployment safety while preserving permissionless innovation across decentralized networks.",
